    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1092">

                <pb facs="1250" n="1106" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg1106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.11241/13: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-04-09T12:00:00+01:00">April 9,
                        1926—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 9—10:35 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>72. Hamburg customs authorities have informed Consulate that, under a new ruling,
                    goods cannot be admitted duty free for consuls already in the country and that
                    duty must be paid on goods previously so admitted including automobiles. Foreign
                    Office informs Embassy <pb facs="1251" n="1107" xml:id="pg1107"/> that German
                    Government is ready to accept on a basis of reciprocity whatever interpretation
                    the American Government cares to put on article 27 of recent treaty<anchor
                        n="86" xml:id="d1092-f1"/> and that it cabled the German Ambassador to take
                    question up with the Department but has not yet had an answer.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
